<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.EmptyState"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             HorizontalAlignment="Center"
             VerticalAlignment="Center"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False"
             IsHitTestVisible="False"
             MaxWidth="{StaticResource WidthEmptyState}">

    <!--
        WHAT THE MIDDLE OF THE WINDOW SAYS WHEN IT HAS NO ROWS. Complaint 9 of the eleven, and the
        one the line under the list could not answer: an empty rectangle does not say whether
        nothing matched or something broke, and a line of grey text below the grid is read after
        the grid, if at all.

        It sits OVER the grid rather than instead of it, so the column headings stay where they
        are - a list whose headings vanish reads as a window that fell over, and at the empty state
        the rows come back on the next key press.

        Five states rather than four, and the fifth is why this is worked out in ListState rather
        than here: a machine that hands over nothing is not a query that matched nothing, and
        offering to clear an empty query would be the window being confidently wrong.

        ITS OWN FILE SINCE 2026-08-18, AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT ASKED IS WORTH KEEPING. MainWindow
        stood at 403 lines against a markup ceiling of 403, which may only ever go down - so the
        operations packet could not add a single line to it, exactly as the details panel could not
        on 2026-08-13. The criterion for where this goes was already written down then: a theme file
        describes the look of ONE CONTROL, and a part of the window lives in its own file beside
        MainWindow.xaml. That entry names the empty state by name as the next case it fits, so this
        is that criterion applied rather than a new one invented to justify a move.

        THREE DEFAULTS ARE TURNED OFF HERE THAT THE STACKPANEL NEVER HAD, and leaving any of them
        would be a fault nothing in the build would report. A UserControl is a ContentControl, and
        a ContentControl arrives Focusable and IsTabStop - measured on this product on 2026-08-13,
        `docs/10` trap 8 - so without the two attributes above this becomes a tab stop that does
        nothing, in front of the list. IsHitTestVisible is the third and it is not a default at
        all: the panel covers the middle of the grid, so without it the rows underneath stop taking
        a click at exactly the moment somebody is trying to get back to them.

        Bound to what the window SAYS rather than to the whole model, the same narrowing the
        details panel makes: nothing here can reach the list, the query or the reading, and what it
        may say is exactly what Says holds.
    -->

    <UserControl.Style>
        <Style TargetType="UserControl">
            <Style.Triggers>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding ListMessage}" Value="">
                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                </DataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>
    </UserControl.Style>

    <StackPanel>

        <TextBlock x:Name="MessageText"
                   Text="{Binding ListMessage}"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   TextAlignment="Center"
                   Style="{StaticResource EmptyStateText}" />

        <!--
            The way out, and it is the only place in this window that says these two keys exist.
            Nielsen's third heuristic - `docs/11` 9.2 names it as this product's weakest - and both
            keys are invisible everywhere else. Empty when there is none to offer, which is the
            loading state and a machine with nothing on it.
        -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="WayOutText"
                   Text="{Binding ListWayOut}"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   TextAlignment="Center"
                   Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}">
            <TextBlock.Style>
                <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource SubduedText}">
                    <Style.Triggers>
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding ListWayOut}" Value="">
                            <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                    </Style.Triggers>
                </Style>
            </TextBlock.Style>
        </TextBlock>

    </StackPanel>

</UserControl>
